STEPConstruct_AP203Context: no <pwd.h>, no user database and no timezone tables on wasm32-wasip1.

Two of the three sites EXTEND a condition that is already there. Upstream excludes Windows, Android
and Emscripten from the user-database surface, and spells the same test twice:

  * `#if !defined(_WIN32) && !defined(__ANDROID__) && !defined(__EMSCRIPTEN__)` around
    `#include <pwd.h>` at :63 gains `&& !defined(__wasi__)`
  * the same condition around the `getpwnam()` block in DefaultPersonAndOrganization() at :178
    gains it too

The third site joins the `#if`/`#elif`/`#else` chain DefaultDateAndTime() already uses to pick a UTC
offset, as a new `#elif defined(__wasi__)` arm inside that one structure rather than as a block
beside it. No arm in the chain fits WASI: `_MSC_VER` is Windows, the `__FreeBSD__` arm is gated on a
version macro upstream will retire (and reaching the same zero by a different route would make WASI
depend on that gate outliving it), and the `#else` reads `timezone`, which wasi-libc keeps inside
`__wasilibc_unmodified_upstream` under the comment "WASI has no timezone tables", along with
`tzset()`, `tzname` and `daylight`.

Guarding the include alone is not enough here, twice over, and each was found by compiling rather
than by reading: a missing header is a fatal diagnostic, so closing `<pwd.h>` uncovered `getpwnam()`
at :181, and closing that uncovered `timezone` at :125. Three sites, one file, one error at a time.

WHAT THE WASI BRANCH RETURNS:

  * DefaultPersonAndOrganization() builds its person from `OSD_Process::UserName()` with no gecos
    lookup, which is what the Windows, Android and Emscripten arms already do. On WASI `UserName()`
    returns an empty string (`wasi-osd-process.patch`) and `OSD_Host::InternetAddress()` returns one
    too (`wasi-osd-host.patch`), so the record written is ORGANIZATION id `IP`, name `Unspecified`,
    and PERSON id `IP,` with an empty first and last name. `wasi-osd-process.patch`'s own header
    already names this call site and records that outcome: a STEP file written from wasm carries an
    empty author.
  * `"Unknown"` was considered for the empty-user-name case and rejected. It is the `else` arm of
    the very block WASI now skips, so keeping it would mean nesting a second condition inside a
    block WASI does not enter, which is the parallel structure `docs/WASI_GUARD_SITES.md` rules out.
    It would also diverge from Emscripten, where an empty user name already yields an empty name.
    An empty name says the runtime has no user; `Unknown` is a given name that no person has, in a
    PERSON record a consumer may display or index.
  * DefaultDateAndTime() reports a UTC offset of exactly zero, so `ahead_or_behind` is
    `StepBasic_aobExact` and both hour and minute components are 0. This is not a fallback. A
    wasip1 instance has no local zone, and `OSD_Process::SystemDate()`, which supplies the calendar
    date and local time beside this offset, reads `localtime()`, which wasi-libc resolves to UTC for
    the same reason. The timestamp and the offset therefore agree; any other offset would make them
    disagree.

WHY THAT IS SAFE FOR EVERY IN-TREE CALLER:

The class has exactly one in-tree consumer, `STEPConstruct_ContextTool`, which holds one by value as
`theAP203` and, only when `IsAP203()` (that is, `write.step.schema` is AP203), appends its creator,
design owner, design supplier, classification officer, security, creation date, classification date,
approval, approver and approval date-time to the model. Nothing reads any of those values back,
parses them, or requires a name to be non-empty: they are written to the file as they stand. Both
defaults are settable as well, through `SetDefaultPersonAndOrganization()` and
`SetDefaultDateAndTime()`, so an application that does have an identity or a zone supplies it and
never reaches either branch.

This is not an incidental file. `STEPConstruct_ContextTool` references it unconditionally and the
STEP writer pulls that, so before this patch a module that writes STEP did not link: 20 undefined
symbols, every one a member of this class, all referenced from `STEPConstruct_ContextTool.cxx.obj`.
Measured in `Scripts/repro/2174/README.md` and asserted by `Scripts/repro/2174/run.sh link`.

VERIFIED, other than by the WASI build: preprocessing the pre-image and the post-image of this file
for native macOS with otherwise identical flags produces an identical token stream, so none of the
three guards changes anything on any platform that was already compiling it.

No `Applies-After:` trailer: no carried patch in `Scripts/patches/` touches this file.

Issue: https://github.com/SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift/issues/2266

